      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.066,
            0.374,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.12,
            0.885,
            0.164
          ],
          "content": "**Adding fractions with the same fractional unit or denominator**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.179,
            0.533,
            0.207
          ],
          "content": "**Example**: Find the sum of $\\frac{2}{5}$ and $\\frac{1}{5}$ ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.226,
            0.885,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": "Let us represent both using the rectangular strips. In both fractions, the fractional unit is the same $\\frac{1}{5}$ , so, each strip will be divided into 5 equal parts."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.305,
            0.485,
            0.333
          ],
          "content": "So $\\frac{2}{5}$ will be represented as —"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.314,
            0.341,
            0.602,
            0.381
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_25_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.399,
            0.504,
            0.426
          ],
          "content": "And $\\frac { 1 } { 5 }$ will be represented as —"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.314,
            0.431,
            0.601,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_25_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.477,
            0.884,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": "Adding the two given fractions is the same as finding out the total number of shaded parts, each of which represent the same fractional unit $\\frac { 1 } { 5 }$ ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.553,
            0.885,
            0.625
          ],
          "content": "In this case, the total number of shaded parts is 3. Since, each shaded part represents the fractional unit $\\frac{1}{5}$ , we see that the 3 shaded parts together represent the fraction $\\frac{3}{5}$ ."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.646,
            0.394,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, $\\frac{2}{5} + \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{3}{5} ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.423,
            0.635,
            0.71,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_25_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.69,
            0.552,
            0.718
          ],
          "content": "**Example**: Find the sum of $\\frac { 4 } { 7 }$ and ${ \\frac { 6 } { 7 } } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.722,
            0.884,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": "Let us represent both again using the rectangular strip model. Here in both fractions, the fractional unit is the same, i.e., $\\frac { 1 } { 7 }$ , so each strip will be divided into 7 equal parts."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.795,
            0.537,
            0.82
          ],
          "content": "Then $\\frac { 4 } { 7 }$ will be represented as —"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.824,
            0.605,
            0.864
          ],
          "content": "",
